{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"90813565-53a1-48ec-be5e-69bf8e3853a5","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A definite integral measures signed area between a curve and the horizontal axis. Start with this positive function.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Integral as signed area"},{"op":"axes","id":"plot","region":"right","x":{"min":0,"max":3,"label":"x"},"y":{"min":0,"max":4,"label":"y"}},{"op":"curve","id":"function","axes_id":"plot","expr":"x+1","domain":[0,3]},{"op":"equation","id":"rule","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x+1"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"90813565-53a1-48ec-be5e-69bf8e3853a5","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Focus on the interval from zero to two. These vertical boundaries enclose the area we want.","ops":[{"op":"curve","id":"boundary","axes_id":"plot","expr":"1+1000*(x-2)","domain":[1.999,2.002]},{"op":"equation","id":"target","anchor":{"el":"rule","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A=\\int_0^2(x+1)\\,dx"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"90813565-53a1-48ec-be5e-69bf8e3853a5","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Approximate the area with rectangles. Each rectangle contributes its height times its width; narrower rectangles improve the approximation.","ops":[{"op":"diagram","id":"rectangles","region":"A3","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.08,0.88],[0.92,0.88]],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"rect","from":[0.12,0.67],"to":[0.3,0.88],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"rect","from":[0.3,0.56],"to":[0.48,0.88],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"rect","from":[0.48,0.45],"to":[0.66,0.88],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"rect","from":[0.66,0.34],"to":[0.84,0.88],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.12,0.67],[0.84,0.23]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"Increasing function above left-endpoint rectangles"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sum","anchor":{"el":"target","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"A=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i^*)\\,\\Delta x"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"For this increasing function, do left-endpoint rectangles underestimate or overestimate the area?","expected_gist":"They underestimate because each rectangle lies below the curve."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"90813565-53a1-48ec-be5e-69bf8e3853a5","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Here the exact region is a trapezoid: width two, with heights one and three. Its area, and therefore the integral, is four.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"sum","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\int_0^2(x+1)\\,dx=\\frac{1+3}{2}\\cdot2=4"},{"op":"text","id":"arealabel","anchor":{"el":"plot","side":"below","gap":0.04},"content":"Area on [0, 2] = 4 square units"}],"checkpoint":null}}
